@@ -0,0 +1,27 @@
+#!/usr/bin/env bash
+set -uo pipefail
+
+if (( $# < 2 )); then
+  echo "usage: $0 LOGFILE COMMAND [ARG ...]" >&2
+  exit 64
+fi
+
+logfile=$1
+shift
+
+{
+  printf 'COMMAND:'
+  printf ' %q' "$@"
+  printf '\n'
+  printf 'START_UTC: %s\n' "$(date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ)"
+} > "$logfile"
+
+"$@" > >(tee -a "$logfile") 2> >(tee -a "$logfile" >&2)
+status=$?
+
+{
+  printf '\nEXIT_STATUS: %d\n' "$status"
+  printf 'END_UTC: %s\n' "$(date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ)"
+} >> "$logfile"
+
+exit "$status"

codex
I found an important command-level issue in my isolated run: selecting only `SPEC.sum-product` removes the loop-invariant circularity from the proof set, so the backend is unrolling a symbolic list rather than checking the candidate’s intended two-claim proof. I’m stopping this diagnostic run and will execute the entry together with its required invariant, while retaining the interrupted log separately so it cannot be mistaken for candidate failure.
